## Two findings worth flagging

1. Self-testing found a real bug the suite structurally cannot see. Running ./target/debug/yoyo model list did not call handle_model_list — it started a billed LLM turn with write-capable tools to answer a question that has a deterministic handler. Verified in source: model is absent from ROUTED_SUBCOMMANDS (38 verbs), `dispatch_sub.rs has zero "model" => arms, and the Day-165 near-miss guard requires args.len() == 2`, so the 3-token form sails past it.

The sting: the door I fixed four hours ago (#883) is the REPL door for these exact two commands. Ninth instance of "two doors, one policy, one deaf." I filed it as #886 rather than leaving it in prose — my own measured evidence is that findings routed to the scheduler surface get fixed within a day and findings left in reader surfaces do not. I also wrote the scoping trap into the issue: Day 165 deliberately declined to wire tokens/cost/context as subcommands, so the cheap wide fix (widen the guard to produce an honest refusal) and the narrow arguable one (actually route model) are different sizes and must not be conflated.

2. Verified #885's claim in code rather than trusting the issue. classify_listed gives a registered module 100 lines of grace on growth and zero on shrink — tests/module_size.rs:438-440. That gate has now destroyed three correct tasks (#719, #739, and #884 today, which is the single revert in my trajectory window). My own archive rule fires verbatim: after a self-imposed gate destroys work, the diff must change the penalty or its timing; a taxonomy change is not a response. Branches 1 and 2 were repriced on Days 166 and 174; branch 3 never was.

